# Feature specs are TRACKED (downstream products commit their specs;
# the framework's own dev specs are stripped at template release by
# sanitize-for-template.sh)

# Deprecated items (kept locally for reference, not in template)
**/.deprecated/

# Backlog index (DERIVED from backlog.md / features/*/plan.md / specs/*/tasks.md
# by .logic-loom/scripts/bash/build-backlog-index.sh). Deliberately UNTRACKED,
# and the DISTINCTION from the dashboard is the point:
#
#   * The INDEX is a MACHINE INTERMEDIATE with no standalone reader. Nobody
#     opens it; it exists to be fed to the renderer. Nothing is lost by
#     regenerating it on demand, so there is no reason to accept the staleness
#     cost of tracking it — and nothing to gate, because there is no committed
#     copy that could disagree with the sources.
#   * The DASHBOARD (artifacts/backlog-dashboard.html) is the thing a HUMAN
#     looks at, so it is TRACKED. See the note below.
#
# Only the thing a human opens becomes tracked. Regenerate this one on demand;
# never commit it. (tests/contract/test_backlog_index.sh asserts this ignore
# rule is still in force.)
.logic-loom/backlog-index.json

# NOTE — artifacts/backlog-dashboard.html is deliberately NOT listed here.
#
# It used to be. It was gitignored, which meant it existed only in whichever
# checkout last ran the generator: the maintainer went looking for it in their
# main checkout and it was not there, twice. Work here happens on worktrees and
# feature branches and always lands on dev-main, and dev state has to be
# consistent across that line — so the page is now TRACKED and flows
# worktree → feature branch → dev-main like any other file. Customers never
# receive it: `artifacts` is a WHOLESALE strip entry in
# .logic-loom/scripts/bash/template-strip-manifest.txt, so the whole directory is
# removed when the sanitized template is built.
#
# The mechanism is "NOT IGNORED + STRIPPED AT PROMOTE" — not a per-branch ignore
# rule. .gitignore is NOT branch-scoped in any reliable way: it is itself a
# tracked file, so a per-branch divergence in it is just a normal file
# difference that merges, conflicts, and silently propagates in whichever
# direction the merge went. Do not try to solve this by giving dev-main and main
# different .gitignore contents.
#
# The cost of tracking a derived artifact is real — it goes stale the moment a
# source is edited without regenerating, the class that has bitten this repo
# three times (dead scrub rules, orphaned test suites, a manifest advertising a
# stripped command). A warn-only lint does not hold; that IS the manifest bug.
# The cost is paid by a FAIL-CLOSED gate:
#     .logic-loom/scripts/bash/check-generated-freshness.sh
# which regenerates the page and fails if the committed copy differs. It runs in
# CI (.github/workflows/plugin-tests.yml). Tracking is licensed by that gate; if
# the gate is ever removed, this file goes back to being ignored.

# Memory search index (generated, regenerable)
.loom-memory-index/
.sdd-memory-index/
vector_helper.py

# Memory working tier (session-specific)
plugins/loom-memory/working/
!plugins/loom-memory/working/.gitkeep
plugins/loom-memory/recall/
!plugins/loom-memory/recall/.gitkeep
plugins/loom-memory/.retention-last-run

# Memory runtime logs (generated)
.docs/memory/search-log.jsonl

# Test artifacts
test-checkpoint-*

# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.egg-info/
.loom-worktree-env

# Local git worktrees (machine-specific; would be committed as a gitlink)
.claude/worktrees/

# Promotion runtime state: the promotion ledger and rehearsal attestations
# written by promote-gate.sh / promotion-record.sh. Per-checkout machine state
# that the gate READS -- not something a human opens -- so it follows the same
# rule as backlog-index.json: track only what a human opens. Tracking it would
# also collide across worktrees, since each checkout promotes independently.
# A project that wants an auditable promotion record should publish it
# deliberately rather than inherit it as tracked noise.
.logic-loom/state/
